-- title:
--   Degree-zero η-sections of the edge family where y(ξ)≠ 0
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}\to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism, where $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ (written `Zp2 p`); $\bar k$ denotes $W(k)/pW(k)$, the quotient by the ideal `pIdeal p (WittVector p k)` generated by $p$, and $\bar\iota$ the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map.
--
--   **The reference module $\Phi$ and its rigidification.** $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $\bar k$ in the sense of the structure `FormalODModule` (a commutative two‑dimensional formal group law with an additive action of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi\circ[a] = [\sigma a]\circ\varpi$). The hypotheses on $\Phi$ are: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\iota$, i.e. the $\bar\iota$‑eigenspaces `lieZero` and `lieOne` of the Lie module are complementary and both invertible; `hΦ4`, that $[p]$ on $\Phi$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that `lieZero` is contained in the kernel of the linear part of $\varpi$ on the Lie module; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ are complementary, where the piece of index $n$ consists of those Cartier module elements on which the Teichmüller action of each $c\in\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ is the homothety by $\bar\iota([c])^{p^n}$. Finally $r_\Phi$ is an additive map from $(\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to\mathbb{Z}_p)$ to the $N$‑module of the graded Cartier module data of $\Phi$ attached to $\bar\iota$ and `hcΦ`, and `hrΦ` requires that for every canonical $L$‑map $L$ on that data, $r_\Phi$ maps the whole of $(\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to\mathbb{Z}_p)$ bijectively onto the degree‑$0$ $\eta$‑piece `etaPiece L … 0` (the intersection of `eta L` with the $N$‑piece of index $0$).
--
--   **The matrix $g$.** $g$ is an element of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$, assumed by `hg` to be the diagonal matrix with entries $p$ and $1$.
--
--   **The edge family.** $E$ denotes the characteristic‑$p$ edge‑chart ring `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` of $\bar k$, with its two distinguished elements $\xi$ and $\eta$, and $X$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$‑module over $E$. The family $\gamma : \mathrm{Fin}\,2\to$ (Cartier module of $X$) is assumed by `hγ` to be a homogeneous $V$‑basis for the structure map $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}\to E$ obtained from $\iota$, the quotient map and the structure morphism $\bar k\to E$ — that is, $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of index $i$ and the determinant of the matrix of tangent vectors of the $\gamma_i$ is a unit — and by `hγa` to have structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p`, the edge constants built from $\xi$ and $\eta$: for all $i$ and $N$ there is $h$ with $\varpi(\gamma_i)$ equal to $\sum_{m<N}V^m$ of the homothety by the $m$‑th constant applied to $\gamma$ at the alternating index, plus $V^N h$. Further, `hXs` asserts that $X$ is special for that structure map and `hX4` that $[p]$ on $X$ has kernel of degree $p^4$.
--
--   **The node point $f_0$ and the isogeny $\rho_0$.** $f_0 : E\to\bar k$ is a ring homomorphism which by `hf₀` is a retraction of the structure morphism $\bar k\to E$ and which by `hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η` kills both $\xi$ and $\eta$. For a natural number $m$ and a two‑variable power series datum $\rho_0$ over $\bar k$, `hρ₀` asserts that $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi\to X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k$ of height $4m$, i.e. an $\mathcal{O}_D$‑homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Write $t_0$ for the rigidified datum `Rigidified.mk` with underlying module $X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k$, index $m$ and reduced isogeny the image of $\rho_0$ in the quotient by $p$. The hypotheses `hOD₀`, `hcb` and `hcΦg` assert respectively that the reduced isogeny of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$‑homomorphism from the base change of $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k$, that the index‑$0$ and index‑$1$ graded pieces of the reduced module of $t_0$ are complementary, and that the same holds for the base change of $\Phi$ (all three taken over $\bar k$ with the identity as further base change). The normalisation hypothesis `hN` provides $a\in\mathbb{N}$ such that for each $i\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, $p^a$ times the value of the rigidification numerator `Rigidified.rigidNum` (formed from $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$, $t_0$, `hOD₀`, `hcb`, `hcΦg`) at the standard basis vector `Pi.single i 1` equals $p^{a+m}$ times: for $i=0$ the class `nMk` of the pair (base change of $\gamma_0$ along $f_0$, then to the quotient and the reduction, $0$); and for $i=1$ the image under `nVarpi` of the corresponding class `nMk` formed from $\gamma_1$.
--
--   **The edge isogeny $\rho_1$.** $\rho_1$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$‑modules over $E$ from $(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k)\otimes_{\bar k}E$ to $X$ whose induced map on Cartier modules is computed on the base‑changed $V$‑basis by `hρ₁0` and `hρ₁1`:
--   $$\rho_1(\gamma_0) = p\cdot\gamma_0 - V\big([\eta^{p^2}]\cdot\gamma_1\big),\qquad \rho_1(\gamma_1) = p\cdot\gamma_1 - V\big([\xi^{p^2}]\cdot\gamma_0\big),$$
--   where $[\,\cdot\,]$ is the Teichmüller lift, $V$ is `verschiebungInt` and $p$ acts through the Witt vectors of $E$; and `hρ₁h` asserts that the underlying series of $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   **The point $y$.** $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$‑algebra, $y : E\to\Omega$ a ring homomorphism, and `hyξ` asserts $y(\xi)\neq 0$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Let $t$ be the rigidified datum over $\Omega$ obtained by pushing along $y$ the datum `Rigidified.mk` with underlying module $X$, index $m+1$, and reduced isogeny the composite of the reduction of $\rho_0$ (base changed to $E$) followed by the reduction of the series of $\rho_1$; let $\psi$ be the composite $W(k)\to\bar k\to E\to\Omega$ of the quotient map, the structure morphism and $y$, and let the further base change be the localisation map `Rigidified.awayHom (1 : Ω)`. Then there exist:
--
--   – a proof `hOD` that the reduced isogeny of $t$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$‑homomorphism from the base change of $\Phi$ along $\psi$ to the reduction of the module of $t$;
--
--   – proofs `hc`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` that the index‑$0$ and index‑$1$ graded pieces are complementary for, respectively, the localised module of $t$, its reduction, and the base change of $\Phi$, all graded by the corresponding structure maps built from $\iota$, $\psi$ and `awayHom (1 : Ω)`;
--
--   – an additive map $L$ from the Cartier module $M$ of the localised module of $t$ to its $N$‑module, together with a proof `hL` that $L$ is a canonical $L$‑map for the graded Cartier module data;
--
--   – elements $z_0,z_1$ of that $N$‑module and proofs `hz₀`, `hz₁` that $z_0$ and $z_1$ are $\eta$‑sections of index $0$ with rigidified coordinate vectors the standard basis vectors $e_0$ and $e_1$ of the standard lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^2\subset\mathbb{Q}_p^2$: that is, each $z_j$ lies in `etaPiece L … 0`, and `LatticeRel` holds for the reduced graded data with index $t.n = m+1$, the rigidification numerator of $t$, the value `etaRed` at $z_j$, and the vector $e_j$;
--
--   – elements $m_0,m_1$ of $M$,
--
--   such that the following four assertions hold: the class of $m_0$ in $M/V(M)$ (the quotient by `vRange`, the image of the Verschiebung) equals the value of the map `u` attached to $L$ at $z_0$, viewed as an element of `eta L` through the first component of the membership given by `hz₀`; likewise the class of $m_1$ in $M/V(M)$ equals the value of `u` at $z_1$; for every coordinate $j\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$ one has
--   $$\mathrm{tangent}(m_1)_j = -\,y(\xi)\cdot\mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j$$
--   with $y(\xi)$ taken in the localisation of $\Omega$ away from $1$; and there exists $j\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$ with $\mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j\neq 0$.
--
--   This is the $\xi\neq 0$ branch of the local analysis of the explicit edge family of special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules of height $4$ in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: at a point $y$ of the edge chart lying off the $\xi=0$ component it produces the two degree-zero $\eta$-sections with rigidified coordinates $e_0$, $e_1$ and records that their tangent vectors are proportional with ratio $-y(\xi)$. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_edge_of_apply_xi_ne_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_edge_of_apply_xi_ne_zero) to identify the kernel lattice attached to such a point.
